[HOLE 1] D2D source-synchronous I/O — give TL_RX and TL_TX a real -min/-max window

WHAT THIS DOES
    tidelink_constraints.sdc:41   set_input_delay  1   ->  -max 2.5  / -min -0.4
    tidelink_constraints.sdc:179  set_output_delay 0.8 ->  -max 0.8  / -min -0.8

    Neither live line carries -max or -min, so SDC applies the single value to
    BOTH ends and the timer sees a source-synchronous interface with a valid
    window of ZERO width. This gives both a window, adds a bind-or-die guard on
    each port bundle, and records where every number came from.

    set_input_delay   1 -> 2   (+1: the -min that did not exist)
    set_output_delay  1 -> 2   (+1: the -min that did not exist)
    new Tcl vars      +4       (D2D_T_UI, D2D_RX_IN_MAX, D2D_RX_IN_MIN,
                                D2D_TX_OUT_SYMM -- all parametric off
                                $D2D_LINK_PERIOD except the two absolutes)
    no clock created, no clock removed, no clock-to-clock exception added.

WHY
    The file has been carrying its own indictment since the pad characterisation
    landed. tidelink_constraints.sdc:699-706:

      "set_input_delay 1 -- carries NEITHER -max NOR -min, so SDC applies 1.0ns
       to both. The tool therefore believes D2D data arrives at exactly
       +1.000ns after the RX clock edge with ZERO valid-window uncertainty. For
       a forwarded-clock source-synchronous interface the window IS the
       constraint, and a min equal to the max is optimistic for hold: it
       asserts the data can never arrive early."

    A missing -min is not symmetric with a missing -max. Optimistic setup fails
    AT SPEED and STA watches the margin shrink; optimistic hold fails at ANY
    speed, is not recoverable by slowing the link, and STA reports it clean.

WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM
    T_UI = 10.0 ns.  DERIVED: one bit per pad-clock edge per lane
        (WavD2DGpioRx_v2.v:400/595/665, SDR), and confirmed against the shipping
        build -- build/full-20260814/reports/cts_clocks.rep:37 gives
        D2D_RX_WORD_CLK_0 period 160.000 waveform {0 80} at ratio 1/16, i.e.
        16 x 10.0 ns.  NOTE the block SDC's T_UI is 4.0 ns, NOT 10.0.

    RX -max 2.5 ns = T_UI/4.  The block SDC's RX_INPUT_DELAY_SYMM ratio
        (tidelink/syn/asic/fusion-compiler/inputs/constraints.sdc:74), SCALED to
        this die's UI. The block's ratio evaluates to 1.0 ns at ITS T_UI=4.0 --
        which is exactly the bare `1` that was on line 41, and that coincidence
        is the defect: an unscaled inheritance asserting a window 2.5x tighter
        than the block ever claimed.

    RX -min -0.4 ns.  ABSOLUTE, NOT SCALED. It is the block SDC's PROVEN value
        (its lines 139-149) and the only -min in this project that has ever
        closed hold: symmetric -1.0 produced "-0.59 ns scen_fast hold WNS / 130
        NVE ... that 11 builds could not work around", and -0.4 fixed it. The
        quantity that set it was the RECEIVING DESIGN'S CLOCK-TREE LATENCY
        (~0.58 ns there), not the link rate, so scaling it with T_UI is a
        category error. This file's own line 705 agrees about what a -min is:
        "clock-to-Q + pad-to-pad mismatch + channel skew".

    TX -max 0.8 ns.  UNCHANGED ON PURPOSE. The block at line ~688 already shows
        0.8 is barely physical and concludes it "cannot be settled from inside
        this repo -- it needs the compute die's CTS report". That is still true.
        Only the SHAPE is fixed here; the magnitude stays theirs.

    TX -min -0.8 ns.  Mirror of -max: symmetric about the launch edge, because
        both io_pad and io_pad_clk leave the serialiser on the same io_clk edge
        (WavD2DGpioTx.v:320/322/326/330). Symmetric is what lets the tool
        balance data against the forwarded clock instead of hold-padding every
        lane -- the 2026-05-05 trap the block SDC's §4 records.

TOOL SUPPORT
    set_input_delay / set_output_delay -min/-max/-add_delay: base SDC, supported
    by Genus 21.1 and Innovus 21.11. Nothing here is vendor-specific and nothing
    here needs a catch wrapper.

