    report from build/full-20260814; none is measured under THIS constraint set.

READ BEFORE JUDGING THE FIRST RUN
    SETUP.  -max +1.5 ns on TL_RX spends 1.5 ns of the `default` path group,
    which closes at WNS +2.435 ns over 4567 paths, 0 violating
    (reports/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads_routed.summary.gz). +2.435 is the WORST
    path in the group, so the floor after this lands is >= +0.935 ns. If the
    group goes negative, the group's margin moved since 08-14 -- re-measure
    before touching the ratio.

    HOLD.  Baseline is post-route-opt hold WNS -0.001 / TNS -0.002 / FEP 7
    (reports/hold_05_route_opt.rep). NEW hold endpoints on TL_RX capture and
    TL_TX launch are this constraint WORKING. Ladder if the count is
    disproportionate: RX -min -0.4 -> -0.2 -> 0.0. TIGHTEN, never delete --
    deleting restores min == max and the optimism.

    DO NOT follow the block SDC's own guard note, which says to "widen it (e.g.
    +/-T_UI/2)" on a hold explosion. That is the wrong sign: a more negative
    -min makes hold strictly harder. The same file's §1 records the fix that
    actually worked and it was the opposite direction.

--- a/ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc
+++ b/ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc
@@ -38,7 +38,105 @@
 set D2D_LINK_PERIOD $EXTCLK_PERIOD
 create_clock -name "D2D_RX_CLK_0" -period "$D2D_LINK_PERIOD"  -waveform "0 [expr $D2D_LINK_PERIOD/2]" [get_ports TL_CLK_RX]
 
-set_input_delay 1 -clock [get_clocks "D2D_RX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}]
+# ── D2D RX EYE — SYMMETRIC SOURCE-SYNCHRONOUS WINDOW (added 2026-08-18) ──────
+# WAS: `set_input_delay 1 -clock D2D_RX_CLK_0 [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}]`, carrying
+# NEITHER -max NOR -min. SDC then applies 1.0ns to BOTH ends, so the timer
+# believes D2D data arrives at exactly +1.000ns after the RX clock edge with
+# ZERO valid-window uncertainty. The pad-characterisation block at the bottom
+# of this file (~line 699) has said so since it landed: "a min equal to the max
+# is optimistic for hold: it asserts the data can never arrive early."
+#
+# WHICH HALF MATTERS. An optimistic -max is a part that fails AT SPEED, and STA
+# can watch the margin shrink. A missing -min is a part that fails at ANY speed,
+# is not recoverable by slowing the link down, and STA reports it CLEAN. Of the
+# two numbers below the -min is the safety-critical one; the -max change is a
+# consistency fix that happens to travel with it.
+
+# --- T_UI, DERIVED NOT ASSUMED ----------------------------------------------
+# The unit interval IS the pad-clock period. Each lane's word clock is
+# io_link_clk = ~count[3] off a free-running 4-bit counter clocked by io_pad_clk
+# (WavD2DGpioRx_v2.v:400/595/665), so exactly one bit is captured per pad-clock
+# edge per lane -- SDR, not DDR. Confirmed against the shipping build rather
+# than inferred: build/full-20260814/reports/cts_clocks.rep:37 reports
+# D2D_RX_WORD_CLK_0 at period 160.000 ns, waveform {0 80}, ratio 1/16 of
+# D2D_RX_CLK_0. 160/16 = 10.0 ns = $D2D_LINK_PERIOD = one UI.
+set D2D_T_UI $D2D_LINK_PERIOD
+
+# --- -max: T_UI/4 -----------------------------------------------------------
+# The SAME RATIO TideLink's own block SDC signs the RX eye off with:
+#   tidelink/syn/asic/fusion-compiler/inputs/constraints.sdc:74
+#   set RX_INPUT_DELAY_SYMM [expr {$T_UI_NS / 4.0}]   ;# +/-25% of UI
+# SCALED, NOT COPIED, and the difference is the whole defect. That file runs
+# T_UI_NS = 4.0 (its line 71), so its number evaluates to 1.0ns -- which is
+# exactly the bare `1` that was on this line. The chiplet's T_UI is 10.0ns, so
+# the same ratio is 2.5ns: the inherited literal was asserting an arrival window
+# 2.5x TIGHTER than the block level ever claimed, in the optimistic direction.
+#
+# COST, MEASURED NOT GUESSED. Raising -max by 1.5ns spends 1.5ns of setup on
+# every TL_RX capture path. Those paths live in Innovus's `default` path group,
+# which in build/full-20260814 closes at WNS +2.435ns over 4567 paths with 0
+# violating (reports/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads_routed.summary.gz, "Setup mode /
+# default" column). +2.435 is the WORST path in that group, so a uniform 1.5ns
+# tightening leaves >= +0.935ns. Affordable on this build; re-check it if the
+# group's margin has moved.
+set D2D_RX_IN_MAX [expr {$D2D_T_UI / 4.0}]
+
+# --- -min: -0.4ns, AN ABSOLUTE, DELIBERATELY NOT SCALED WITH T_UI -----------
+# READ THIS BEFORE "FIXING" IT TO -$D2D_RX_IN_MAX FOR SYMMETRY.
+#
+# 1. IT IS NOT A UI FRACTION AND MUST NOT BE ONE. The block SDC's -min carries
+#    the one comment in either file that is a MEASUREMENT rather than a budget
+#    (tidelink/syn/asic/fusion-compiler/inputs/constraints.sdc:139-149): the
+#    symmetric -1.0ns "combined with the aspect-2.0 clock-tree latency (~0.58 ns
+#    from pad_clk_rx to capture FF) produced a -0.59 ns scen_fast hold WNS / 130
+#    NVE failure ... that 11 builds could not work around", and only loosening
+#    -min to -0.4 fixed it. The quantity that set -0.4 was the RECEIVING
+#    DESIGN'S CLOCK-TREE LATENCY, not the link rate. Multiplying it by 2.5
+#    because our UI is 2.5x longer would be a category error.
+#
+# 2. THE PHYSICS AGREES, AND SO DOES THIS FILE. Line ~705 below: "The launch
+#    pads are the same cell for clock and data on both dies, so the residual is
+#    clock-to-Q + pad-to-pad mismatch + channel skew, which is where a real -min
+#    belongs." Those terms are hundreds of picoseconds on a wirebond D2D link
+#    and they do not grow when the link slows down. -0.4ns is a mismatch
+#    allowance standing in for a link budget that does not exist yet.
+#
+# 3. WE HAVE NO HEADROOM TO BE MORE GENEROUS. A negative -min buys hold
+#    pressure one-for-one: the pad->flop route must supply
+#    (clock insertion + t_hold + |min|). Our RX clock insertion is at least as
+#    large as the block's 0.58ns -- this file quotes 1.09ns at line 698
+#    (pnr_m7.log:81871), and build/full-20260814's own
+#    reports/cts_clock_latency_{setup,hold}.rep put the D2D_RX_CLK_0 network
+#    latency at +0.171 (late) and -0.395 (early), a ~0.57ns spread across views.
+#    Nothing in that says we can afford MORE than the block could.
+#
+# FALLBACK LADDER IF HOLD BLOWS UP: -0.4 -> -0.2 -> 0.0. TIGHTEN (reduce the
+# magnitude), never delete -- deleting restores min==max and the optimism.
+#
+# AND NOTE, BECAUSE IT IS WRONG AND IT WILL BE QUOTED AT YOU: the block SDC's
+# own hold-trap guard (its lines 130-136) says that if hold endpoints jump
+# "toward ~100+ ... the symmetric window is still too tight -- widen it (e.g.
+# +/-T_UI/2)". That is the WRONG SIGN. Widening means a MORE negative -min,
+# which makes hold strictly HARDER by the equation above. The same file's own
+# §1 records the fix that actually worked and it was the opposite: -1.0 -> -0.4.
+# Follow the recorded fix, not the guard note.
+set D2D_RX_IN_MIN -0.4
+
+# BIND OR DIE, same doctrine as the word-clock anchors above. If TL_RX ever
+# stops matching 8 ports, a silently-empty collection leaves the RX eye
+# unconstrained behind two lines that read as though it were handled.
+if {[sizeof_collection [get_ports -quiet {TL_RX[*]}]] != 8} {
+    error "tidelink_constraints.sdc: TL_RX\[*\] matched\
+           [sizeof_collection [get_ports -quiet {TL_RX[*]}]] ports, expected 8.\
+           The D2D receive eye would be left unconstrained. Refusing to continue."
+}
+
+# -add_delay on BOTH, so the -min does not replace the -max. Without it the
+# second set_input_delay on the same port/clock pair removes the first, and the
+# file would read as a window while behaving as a single value -- the exact
+# class of defect this block exists to remove.
+set_input_delay -max $D2D_RX_IN_MAX -clock [get_clocks "D2D_RX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}] -add_delay
+set_input_delay -min $D2D_RX_IN_MIN -clock [get_clocks "D2D_RX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_RX[*]}] -add_delay
 
 #-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # THE LINK-CLOCK DIVIDER, AND WHY THE TX CONSTRAINTS STILL HOLD
@@ -176,7 +274,51 @@
 
 create_generated_clock -name "D2D_TX_CLK_0" -source [get_pins u_nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip/u_soc/u_tidelink/u_chiplet_controller/u_wlink/pad_clk_tx] -divide_by 1 [get_ports TL_CLK_TX]
 
-set_output_delay 0.8 -clock [get_clocks "D2D_TX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_TX[*]}]
+# ── D2D TX EYE — SYMMETRIC WINDOW ABOUT THE LAUNCH EDGE (added 2026-08-18) ───
+# WAS: `set_output_delay 0.8 -clock D2D_TX_CLK_0 [get_ports {TL_TX[*]}]` -- same
+# defect as the RX line, opposite direction. With neither -max nor -min, SDC
+# applies +0.8ns to BOTH, which asserts that the far die's external path has
+# ZERO variation: it can never be faster than 0.8ns and never slower. A +0.8ns
+# -min is a HOLD GIFT -- it tells the timer that 0.8ns of external delay always
+# exists to pad the far flop's hold requirement. Nothing off this die supplies
+# that guarantee.
+#
+# THE VALUE 0.8 IS NOT CHANGED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The block at line ~688
+# below already dissects it -- the far PDDW04DGZ_G RX pad alone burns 0.675ns,
+# leaving 0.125ns for the far die's routing and setup, and it concludes: "It
+# cannot be settled from inside this repo -- it needs the compute die's CTS
+# report." That is still true. Inventing a scaled number here would be
+# substituting our arithmetic for their measurement. What IS settleable from
+# inside this repo is the SHAPE, and the shape was wrong.
+#
+# WHY SYMMETRIC ABOUT THE LAUNCH EDGE, rather than an asymmetric absolute
+# window. Both io_pad and io_pad_clk leave the WavD2DGpioTx serialiser on the
+# same io_clk edge (WavD2DGpioTx.v:320/322/326/330 -- the pad clock is a
+# WavClockGate on io_clk, the data leaves the same net), so the transmit
+# relationship is EDGE-ALIGNED. A symmetric window lets the tool balance
+# TL_TX[*] against TL_CLK_TX; an asymmetric one makes it hold-pad every lane
+# instead. That is the 2026-05-05 trap the block SDC records at its §4
+# ("Why not the 2026-05-05 trap") and it applies here unchanged.
+set D2D_TX_OUT_SYMM 0.8
+
+if {[sizeof_collection [get_ports -quiet {TL_TX[*]}]] != 8} {
+    error "tidelink_constraints.sdc: TL_TX\[*\] matched\
+           [sizeof_collection [get_ports -quiet {TL_TX[*]}]] ports, expected 8.\
+           The D2D transmit eye would be left unconstrained. Refusing to continue."
+}
+
+set_output_delay -max  $D2D_TX_OUT_SYMM -clock [get_clocks "D2D_TX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_TX[*]}] -add_delay
+set_output_delay -min -$D2D_TX_OUT_SYMM -clock [get_clocks "D2D_TX_CLK_0"] [get_ports {TL_TX[*]}] -add_delay
+
+# WHAT THIS COSTS, SO THE FIRST RUN IS NOT MISREAD. -max is unchanged, so setup
+# on TL_TX is unchanged. -min goes +0.8 -> -0.8, a 1.6ns swing IN THE HOLD
+# DIRECTION ONLY, and it is the first real hold requirement these 8 ports have
+# ever carried. Expect NEW hold endpoints on the TX launch flops. Baseline to
+# compare against: build/full-20260814 post-route-opt hold WNS -0.001 / TNS
+# -0.002 / FEP 7 (reports/hold_05_route_opt.rep). New hold endpoints here are
+# the constraint WORKING, not the constraint being wrong; they are ordinary
+# buffer-insertion work on 8 nets. See the ladder on the RX -min above if the
+# count is disproportionate.
 
 # CLOCK UNCERTAINTY — was ABSENT for both D2D clocks, so they were timed with
 # ZERO margin on setup and hold. Verified on the 2026-08-06 run: paths in these
